Z||000003||Brucepts||07-03-2001||09:59 AM||||Clean up the casting flash in the port area. But DO NOT make the port bigger other than gasket matching it. Three angle seat cut on the intake and two angles on the ext, keeping the seat face wider .045-.050 (int) .055-.060 (ext) this will aid in smoothing the flow over the seat and allow good heat transfer. Back cut the valves after finding the seat width on the face. A three angle will help low lift flow numbers up to .150" it will not aid flow in higher rpm engines past this point as much. Flowbench data has shown almost double the cfm's at low lift on other heads I have flowed. I have not had time to play with the Duke head as of yet, will have supporting data in the very near future with specific numbers as soon as I get some free time to "play". I first need to find a few "donor" heads at the scrapyard I can hack-up and play with.<P>------------------<BR>Advanced Dealer in the Great Material Continuum . . .<BR>Non Fiero Website: <A HREF="http://tractorsport.com" TARGET=_blank>http://tractorsport.com</A> <BR>You can't race a dyno . . .||||reg||1
Z||000004||baldlobo||07-03-2001||11:27 AM||||compettion camshaft specs.<BR>                                                      <BR>PART # 52-115-4<BR>GRIND NUMBER: P4 240H-8<BR>ENGINE: PONTIAC 4 CYL 151<BR>                 INTAKE   EXHAUST<BR>VALVE ADJUSTMENT    HYD      HYD<BR>GROSS VALVE LIFT   .455     .455                      <BR>.006  TAPPET LIFT   240      248                      <BR>VALVE TIMING     OPEN     CLOSE                       <BR>AT  .006 INT     12 BTDC  48 ABDC                     <BR>         EXH     52 BBDC  16 ATDC                     <BR>THESE SPECS ARE FOR CAM INSTALLED                     <BR>AT  108  INTAKE CENTER LINE                           <BR>                  INTAKE  EXHAUST                     <BR>DURATION AT .050    192      200                      <BR>LOBE LIFT         .2600    .2600                      <BR>LOBE SEPARATION     108   <P><BR>does (1) 102 hp @ 4000-4500 rpm and 148 ft. lbs. of torque @ 3000 rpm. on desk top dyno<P>also Stock motor with header and ported heads.<BR>132 hp @ 5000 rpm & 159 ft. lbs. of torque @ 3500-4000 rpm.<P>and thanks "formula" he's the one who knows about porting the head.||||reg||1
Z||000005||88formula||07-03-2001||10:35 PM||||Performing a performance valve job, cleaning up the intake/exhaust runners and back cutting the valves is a real good way to not only increase the engines torque but horsepower too.  What Brucepts said is good advice.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
